<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Ford</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Ford was an American industrialist and engineer who founded the Ford Motor Company in 1903 and is credited with making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobiles</a> affordable for middle-class Americans through the system known as Fordism. Born on a farm in Greenfield Township, Michigan, he introduced the Ford Model T in 1908, credited with revolutionising transportation and American industry, and was among the pioneers of the five-day work-week. In the 1920s he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> through his newspaper The Dearborn Independent and the book The International Jew. He turned the company over to his grandson Henry Ford II in 1945 and left most of his wealth to the Ford Foundation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-ford.jpg" alt="Henry Ford" width="330" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fred Hartsook · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_ford_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Henry Ford</b> reported that absenteeism in his companies had halved, and alcohol consumption fell dramatically.</blockquote><a href="/movement/temperance-movement">Temperance movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Economic antisemitism rests on the claim that Jews perform harmful economic activities, above all that they control the world’s finances; the forged Protocols of the Elders of Zion promoted this, and <b>Henry Ford</b> repeated it.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/antisemitism">Antisemitism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Investors reorganised <b>Henry Ford</b>'s former company as Cadillac, named after the founder of Detroit.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry Ford</b> received a patent for a car body built from plastic panels on a tubular frame, part of his experiments with materials derived from soybeans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-13">13 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry Ford</b> and eleven investors founded the company in Detroit.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-16">16 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
